---
// Homepage v2 (2026-07-03) — "Plain Words, Same Teeth". The 2026-06
// Fleet homepage carried ~22 sections and spoke WebKit/SOCKS5 to
// non-technical operators in the first screens. This rebuild keeps every
// technical claim but moves each to the altitude where its reader lives:
//   Band A (hero → use cases): plain language, zero unexplained jargon.
//   Band B (mid-page): big plain line + small mono technical line.
//   Band C (// FOR DEVELOPERS + deep pages): full precision.
// 13 sections. Verbatim-preserved proof blocks: the fleet wall, the GUI
// cockpit, the detection matrix, the works-with-your-stack cards, and
// the real SDK snippet. Pricing figures are BOUND to src/data/pricing.ts
// (no hand-typed dollar amounts in this file).
//
// 2026-09-16 — Band-A readability pass (owner: "I don't see much changes
// to website text"). Earlier passes made every claim true but edited
// sentence by sentence, so Band A still read like Band C: long sentences,
// asides stacked inside asides, and terms a first-time visitor has to
// decode. This pass restores the band split instead. Band A now says the
// benefit first and the mechanism second or not at all, in sentences that
// stop; a term only appears where the same sentence explains it (WebKit =
// the software behind Safari on iPhone, fingerprint = what a website
// measures to tell devices apart, concurrent = running at the same time,
// plan family = the kind of plan). The Band-B tiles keep a precise small
// line but no longer try to decode themselves for a beginner — the big
// plain line above carries that. Band C (// for developers, the signal
// table + its footnote, "Apple's engine. Not a Chromium copy.") KEEPS ITS
// PRECISION — it is the one band where full precision is the point — with
// exactly ONE edit in this pass, recorded here because the earlier version
// of this comment claimed the band was untouched and was wrong: the
// developer-band title now reads "Real iPhone Safari." The blanket identity
// claim it replaced (the adjective this page's own guard forbids, in the
// same family as "identical") is retired — precision unchanged, claim
// retired, evidence is per surface and never a blanket identity. The signal
// table, its footnote and every technical row are untouched.
//
// 2026-09-16 (follow-up) — verifier defects fixed, all in Band A:
//   • the behavioural lead no longer implies a per-profile on/off switch
//     that does not exist. The app's own Behavior tab is the source:
//     human-like input is ON BY DEFAULT for every session a profile
//     launches (apps/gui-client ProfilesView; the API's behavioral_profile
//     is an optional persona choice, and omitting it defaults).
//   • the how-it-works card no longer promises session RESUMPTION — a
//     profile persists logins, cookies and history, not open pages.
//   • the first proof card's eyebrow no longer makes an unscoped blanket
//     claim that the card's own body scopes away; it reads "Measured, not
//     guessed", which is what "checked against real iPhones" supports.
//   • SOCKS5 is glossed in plain words like its neighbours in that row.
//   • the pricing lead stops teaching the internal label "plan families".
// Footer.astro moved in the same pass: the site-wide tagline left the
// retired "Pixel-identical" family (and the undefined "GUI") behind.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Card from '../components/Card.astro';
import IconTile from '../components/IconTile.astro';
import Stat from '../components/Stat.astro';
import FeatureRow from '../components/FeatureRow.astro';
import CtaBand from '../components/CtaBand.astro';
import CodeWindow from '../components/CodeWindow.astro';
import AppScreen from '../components/AppScreen.astro';
import { API_TIERS } from '../data/pricing';
import { DEVICE_SUPPORT } from '../data/capabilities';

// 2026-09-11 — every GUI depiction on this page is a REAL capture of the
// desktop app (src/assets/screens/*.png — Playwright shots of the visual
// harness rendering the app's own React components with curated fixture
// data: Amsterdam / Tokyo / Berlin / São Paulo, example hosts, never a real
// session or account). The hand-drawn HTML mockups they replace had drifted
// from the app. Regenerate: scripts/marketing-screens.mjs (README beside
// the assets). The captures are the source of truth for what is SHOWN —
// the alt text and caption strips describe what is in the picture and
// nothing more.
// 2026-09-21 — "Bringing The Stage everywhere" §4/§7: the hero now shows the
// AI view (scene `ai-running`, cropped to `ai-running-hero.png`) — the real,
// lit iPhone a prospect remembers, mid-task, the plan running beside it. The
// former hero capture (the profiles grid) moves to §2 "What is Driftstack?",
// whose Band-B claims ("each iPhone can browse from the country you choose")
// are what its own caption strip already describes — image AND caption move
// together, unchanged, so neither is stranded next to text that doesn't
// match it. `fleetScreen`/`FLEET_ALT` name that relocated pair; `heroScreen`/
// `HERO_ALT` keep their names for the (now AI) hero slot.
import heroScreen from '../assets/screens/ai-running-hero.png';
import fleetScreen from '../assets/screens/profiles-grid-hero.png';
import aiRunningScreen from '../assets/screens/ai-running.png';
import simulatorScreen from '../assets/screens/simulator.png';
import profilesListScreen from '../assets/screens/profiles-list.png';
import proxiesScreen from '../assets/screens/proxies.png';

// W292.B-style data binding: every figure below derives from pricing.ts.
const fmtUsd = (n: number): string => `$${n.toLocaleString('en-US')}`;
const manualLadder = API_TIERS.filter((t) => t.tierType === 'manual');
const apiLadder = API_TIERS.filter((t) => t.tierType === 'api' && t.monthlyUsd !== null);
const manualFrom = fmtUsd(Math.min(...manualLadder.map((t) => t.monthlyUsd!)));
const apiFrom = fmtUsd(Math.min(...apiLadder.map((t) => t.monthlyUsd!)));
const manualLineup = manualLadder.map((t) => `${t.name} ${fmtUsd(t.monthlyUsd!)}/mo`).join(' · ');
const apiLineup = apiLadder.map((t) => `${t.name} ${fmtUsd(t.monthlyUsd!)}/mo`).join(' · ');
const manualCaps = manualLadder.map((t) => t.concurrent).join(' / ');
const apiCaps = apiLadder.map((t) => t.concurrent).join(' / ');

// Alt text for the captures — what each screen SHOWS, in plain words
// (Band A: cities spelled out, no protocol jargon). Kept as constants so the
// parity pin can read them without parsing markup.
const HERO_ALT =
  'The Driftstack desktop app, AI Browser Automation view: the AI running a ' +
  'task on a real iPhone, lit up mid-task, with the six steps it planned ' +
  'beside it — three already done (opened the store, accepted the cookie ' +
  'banner, searched the store), the current step highlighted, and two still ' +
  'to come.';
const FLEET_ALT =
  'The Driftstack desktop app, Profiles view: eight iPhone profile cards, each a ' +
  'small phone showing its name — amsterdam shopper, tokyo sneakers, london ' +
  'checkout, paris fashion and more — the iPhone it runs as, whether it is Live ' +
  'or Idle, the city it browses from (Amsterdam, Tokyo, Zurich, Berlin, London, ' +
  'Paris), the connection it uses, and a Launch button.';
const AI_RUNNING_ALT =
  'The Driftstack desktop app, AI Browser Automation view, mid-task: a real ' +
  'iPhone lit up in its dark stage, showing a shop’s search results, ' +
  'beside the mission bar (task title, a Running status, budget remaining, ' +
  'the profile and model in use) and the six-step plan running alongside it.';
const SIMULATOR_ALT =
  'A floating device window in the Driftstack desktop app, lifted over the app ' +
  'behind it — the Profiles view, its grid of iPhone profile cards showing ' +
  'around the window. The window toolbar names the profile (tokyo sneakers) ' +
  'and the device (iPhone 17) with a Live badge, the same profile the grid ' +
  'behind shows running. On the phone screen an example shop page, searched ' +
  'for a rain jacket, showing four jackets with their prices in euros, and ' +
  'the iPhone keyboard open below it. Beside the screen a panel lists the ' +
  'session link, how fast the video is running, its exit — a residential ' +
  'connection in Tokyo, with the time zone to match — and the device identity ' +
  'readouts.';
const PROFILES_LIST_ALT =
  'The Driftstack desktop app, Profiles view as a list: eight rows sorted by ' +
  'name, one per iPhone profile — its name and iPhone model, tags, Live or Idle ' +
  'status, the exit address and city it browses from, when it was created and ' +
  'last used, storage used, a note, and Launch, Edit, Duplicate, Trim and ' +
  'Delete actions; the live row shows Open session and Stop instead.';
const PROXIES_ALT =
  'The Driftstack desktop app, Proxies view: the header counts three proxies, ' +
  'all healthy, above three editors side by side for your own internet exits — ' +
  'a SOCKS5 proxy with an example host, a WireGuard tunnel showing the server, ' +
  'address and routes it saved, and an OpenVPN tunnel with its config file in ' +
  'the box — each with a Test button and Save changes.';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Driftstack"
  description="A real iPhone browser in the cloud that every website sees as a genuine iPhone — never a bot. Automate iPhone Safari from TypeScript, Python, Go, or a desktop app. Start free."
>
  <!-- ═══ 1 · HERO — Fleet mission control. Band A: the mockup metadata is
       de-jargoned (proxy protocol names → "own connection"; the telemetry
       footer reads like a person wrote it). Honest-claims: only real
       capabilities; generic professional telemetry. ═══ -->
  <section class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <div class="hero-glow" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="absolute inset-0 grid-bg opacity-40" aria-hidden="true"></div>
    <div class="relative mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-16 sm:pb-24 sm:pt-24 md:pt-28">
      <div class="grid gap-10 sm:gap-12 md:grid-cols-2 md:items-center">
        <div class="animate-fade-up">
          <p class="section-label">iPhone Safari · on demand</p>
          <h1
            class="mt-4 text-[1.6rem] font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink sm:mt-5 sm:text-4xl md:text-5xl"
            style="letter-spacing: -0.02em; line-height: 1.12;"
          >
            Your own real iPhones, in the cloud.
          </h1>
          <p class="mt-4 max-w-prose text-sm text-tk-ink-2 leading-relaxed sm:mt-5 sm:text-base md:text-lg">
            Start as many real iPhones as you need. Each one keeps its own
            logins, its own history, and its own location. Drive them by hand,
            by code, or by AI. To every website, they're just
            people on phones.
          </p>
          <div class="mt-5 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3 sm:mt-8 sm:gap-4">
            <a href="/pricing/#free" class="btn-primary">Start free</a>
            <a href="#how-it-works" class="btn-secondary">See how it works</a>
          </div>
          <p class="mt-3 text-xs text-tk-ink-2 sm:mt-4">
            One profile · 20-minute sessions · no card required.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- 2026-09-21 — the AI view mid-task: the real, lit iPhone from the
             desktop app's AI Browser Automation view (scene `ai-running`,
             cropped to the phone + its plan, `ai-running-hero.png`), the
             asset a prospect actually remembers, first thing they see. A
             capture of the app's own components (see AppScreen.astro), not
             a drawing — `accent` gives it AppScreen's rim-and-room "this one
             is lit" treatment. The profiles-grid capture this replaced (the
             literal "command a fleet of real iPhones" visual) moved to §2
             "What is Driftstack?", together with its own caption strip —
             see `fleetScreen` there. Headline + intro paragraph above are
             unchanged copy; only the image changed. -->
        <div class="relative animate-fade-up">
          <AppScreen
            src={heroScreen}
            alt={HERO_ALT}
            priority
            accent
            sizes="(min-width: 768px) 552px, calc(100vw - 48px)"
          />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══ 2 · WHAT IS DRIFTSTACK — Band B tiles: big plain claim + small
       mono technical line. Merges the former stats strip + "What sets us
       apart" differentiator strip (2026-07-03; the no-JS-patching claim
       moved to the proof section's Engine-deep card). ═══ -->
  <Section
    label="What is Driftstack?"
    title="Real iPhones in the cloud, ready in seconds."
    lead="Websites can tell when a desktop browser is pretending to be a phone. Driftstack doesn't pretend. Every session runs a real iPhone browser."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
      <Stat
        plain="A real iPhone, not a lookalike"
        technical="Runs a build of Apple's own WebKit — the engine family behind iPhone Safari"
      />
      <Stat
        plain="Websites see a normal iPhone visitor"
        technical="What a website can measure is checked against real iPhones, check by check"
      />
      <Stat
        plain="Click through it yourself, or drive it from code"
        technical="From code — TypeScript, Python, or Go — or point and click in the desktop app. Same browser either way"
      />
      <Stat
        plain="Each iPhone can browse from the country you choose"
        technical="Attach your own proxy or VPN to a profile — SOCKS5, OpenVPN or WireGuard"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- 2026-09-21 — the fleet visual (profiles-grid) that used to be the
         hero, relocated here unchanged (image + its own caption strip: cities
         spelled out, "own connection") because THIS section's own claim —
         "each iPhone can browse from the country you choose" — is what the
         caption describes; the hero above now shows the AI view instead. Band
         A: the caption stays real copy, outside the data-contrast-decorative
         frame, so the contrast scanner still reads it. -->
    <div class="relative mt-12">
      <AppScreen src={fleetScreen} alt={FLEET_ALT} />
      <p class="mt-4 text-center font-mono text-[10px] text-tk-ink-3">
        🇳🇱 Amsterdam · 🇯🇵 Tokyo · 🇩🇪 Berlin · 🇬🇧 London · 🇫🇷 Paris — each on its own connection
      </p>
      <div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-x-4 gap-y-1 font-mono text-[10px] text-tk-ink-3">
        <span><b class="text-tk-ready-text">8 iPhone profiles</b>, ready to launch</span>
        <span>each with <b class="text-tk-ink-2">its own identity</b></span>
        <span>each on <b class="text-tk-ready-text">its own connection</b></span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ═══ 3 · HOW IT WORKS — plain-language 3 steps (Band A; the hero's
       secondary CTA anchors here). ═══ -->
  <Section
    id="how-it-works"
    label="How it works"
    title="Three steps to a real iPhone in the cloud."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
      <Card title="Pick an iPhone profile">
        <IconTile slot="icon"><span class="font-mono text-base font-semibold">1</span></IconTile>
        Start with the default iPhone, or set up your own. A profile remembers
        its logins, cookies and history, so the next session is still signed
        in.
      </Card>
      <Card title="Start a session">
        <IconTile slot="icon"><span class="font-mono text-base font-semibold">2</span></IconTile>
        One click in the desktop app starts a real iPhone Safari browser in the
        cloud. Your code can start one the same way. No phones to buy, nothing
        to install.
      </Card>
      <Card title="Drive it your way">
        <IconTile slot="icon"><span class="font-mono text-base font-semibold">3</span></IconTile>
        Click around it yourself, or let your code drive it from TypeScript,
        Python or Go. Every website it visits sees a genuine iPhone, never a
        bot.
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ═══ 4 · USE CASES — personas, operators first (Band A). ═══ -->
  <Section
    label="Use cases"
    title="Built for the work you actually do."
  >
    <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-3">
      <Card title="Run many accounts, safely apart">
        <IconTile slot="icon">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M16 21v-2a4 4 0 0 0-4-4H5a4 4 0 0 0-4 4v2" />
            <circle cx="8.5" cy="7" r="4" />
            <path d="M20 8v6M23 11h-6" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        Some websites recognize the device behind an account, and link the
        accounts that share one. Give each account its own profile and it looks
        like its own physical phone, every session, every time. What a website
        measures to tell devices apart is called its
        <a href="/glossary/#fingerprint" class="text-tk-accent-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-tk-accent-2">fingerprint</a>.
        <a
          href="/use-cases/multi-account/"
          class="mt-3 block text-tk-accent-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-tk-accent-2"
          >More →</a
        >
      </Card>
      <Card title="Test on the real thing">
        <IconTile slot="icon">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <path d="M9 11l3 3 8-8" />
            <path d="M21 12v7a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2V5a2 2 0 0 1 2-2h11" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        Your automated tests click through your site the way a real person
        would. Run them on a real iPhone browser instead of a desktop browser
        in a phone costume. The bug you reproduce is the bug your iPhone users
        hit.
        <a
          href="/use-cases/qa-testing/"
          class="mt-3 block text-tk-accent-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-tk-accent-2"
          >More →</a
        >
      </Card>
      <Card title="See what iPhone users see">
        <IconTile slot="icon">
          <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
            <ellipse cx="12" cy="5" rx="9" ry="3" />
            <path d="M3 5v6c0 1.7 4 3 9 3s9-1.3 9-3V5" />
            <path d="M3 11v6c0 1.7 4 3 9 3s9-1.3 9-3v-6" />
          </svg>
        </IconTile>
        Many sites show phones a different page than they show computers. With
        Driftstack you collect the phone version: the prices, the layout and
        the offers an iPhone visitor sees. No bouncing to a "please use our
        app" page.
        <a
          href="/use-cases/web-scraping/"
          class="mt-3 block text-tk-accent-text underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-tk-accent-2"
          >More →</a
        >
      </Card>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ═══ 5 · TWO WAYS TO DRIVE + the floating device window (Band A
       headline; founder 2026-06-12: show the GUI itself, no live-session
       implication — since 2026-09-11 the GUI is shown as a real capture, not
       a hand-drawn cockpit; since 2026-09-12 that capture is the app window
       itself with the device window floating over it). ═══ -->
  <Section
    id="drive-it"
    label="Three ways to drive it"
    title="Point and click — or just say what you want."
    lead="The desktop app keeps every iPhone one click away, in folders and tags you choose. When a job is repetitive, type what you want in plain English. The built-in AI agent does it while you watch. The agent comes with Team plans and up, and with every API plan."
  >
      {/* 2026-09-23 — "One real iPhone. Three ways in." The redesign's
          three-surfaces direction, as an index to this section and the
          developer band: the same phone, driven by hand in the desktop app,
          by the AI agent from a sentence, or by code. Each card says WHAT it
          is for and links to where the page shows it; no capability is
          claimed here that the section it links to does not show. */}
      <div class="ways-in mt-10" aria-labelledby="ways-in-title">
        <p id="ways-in-title" class="text-sm font-semibold text-tk-ink">
          One real iPhone. Three ways in.
        </p>
        <ul class="mt-4 grid gap-4 md:grid-cols-3" role="list">
          <li class="ways-in-card">
            <span class="ways-in-ico" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><rect x="3" y="4" width="18" height="13" rx="2" /><path d="M8 21h8M12 17v4" /></svg>
            </span>
            <span class="ways-in-kicker font-mono">You drive</span>
            <b class="ways-in-name">The desktop app</b>
            <span class="ways-in-body">Point and click, live. Profiles in folders, one click to launch, and any session in its own phone window.</span>
            <a class="ways-in-link" href="#desktop-app">See the app</a>
          </li>
          <li class="ways-in-card" data-lit>
            <span class="ways-in-ico" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M12 3l1.9 5.1L19 10l-5.1 1.9L12 17l-1.9-5.1L5 10l5.1-1.9z" /><path d="M19 17l.8 2.2L22 20l-2.2.8L19 23l-.8-2.2L16 20l2.2-.8z" /></svg>
            </span>
            <span class="ways-in-kicker font-mono">It drives</span>
            <b class="ways-in-name">The AI agent</b>
            <span class="ways-in-body">Say what you want in plain English and watch it happen. It pauses to ask you before buying, paying or deleting.</span>
            <a class="ways-in-link" href="#ai-agent">See it run</a>
          </li>
          <li class="ways-in-card">
            <span class="ways-in-ico" aria-hidden="true">
              <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="20" height="20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.75" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M8 7l-5 5 5 5M16 7l5 5-5 5" /></svg>
            </span>
            <span class="ways-in-kicker font-mono">Your code drives</span>
            <b class="ways-in-name">The API</b>
            <span class="ways-in-body">Official SDKs for TypeScript, Python and Go, or plain HTTPS from anything. No AI cost, no AI wait.</span>
            <a class="ways-in-link" href="#for-developers">See the code</a>
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      {/* The desktop app with its floating device window lifted over it —
          a REAL capture of both (the app's own Profiles view behind, the
          popped-out simulator window over it, an example shop page on the
          phone screen; see AppScreen.astro), replacing the hand-drawn
          cockpit + floating simulator. No live-session implication: the
          harness renders both from fixture data. The explanatory copy below
          stays real copy, outside the decorative frame. */}
      <div id="desktop-app" class="mt-10 scroll-mt-24">
        <AppScreen src={simulatorScreen} alt={SIMULATOR_ALT} />
      </div>

      <p class="mt-10 max-w-prose text-sm text-tk-ink-2">
        The desktop app is home base. Keep profiles in folders, launch one
        with a click, and pop any session out into its own floating phone
        window. Developers can do all of it from code instead.
        <strong class="text-tk-ink">A job written as code runs without the
        AI agent at all: no AI cost, no AI wait.</strong>
      </p>

      <!-- 2026-09-21 — "the built-in AI agent does it while you watch",
           illustrated: the same AI view capture as the hero (scene
           `ai-running`), full frame this time (mission bar included), placed
           right under the sentence it depicts. Not `accent`: that treatment
           stays reserved for the hero, the one capture meant to read as lit
           at first glance. -->
      <div id="ai-agent" class="mt-10 scroll-mt-24">
        <AppScreen src={aiRunningScreen} alt={AI_RUNNING_ALT} />
      </div>
  </Section>

  <!-- ═══ 6 · THE PROOF — "One iPhone among millions." Merges the former
       why-it-works cards + detection matrix + how-it's-built statement +
       comparison teaser into one flagship section (2026-07-03). The
       costume-metaphor lead is the Band-A on-ramp; the matrix + engine
       paragraph carry the full technical teeth. ═══ -->
  <section class="relative border-t border-tk-border">
    <div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-6 py-24">
      <p class="section-label">Why Driftstack works</p>
      <h2
        class="mt-5 text-3xl font-semibold tracking-tight text-tk-ink sm:mt-6 sm:text-5xl md:text-7xl lg:text-8xl"
        style="letter-spacing: -0.04em; line-height: 1.1;"
      >
        One iPhone among millions.
      </h2>
      <p class="mt-6 max-w-prose text-base text-tk-ink-2 leading-relaxed sm:mt-8 md:text-lg">
        Most tools dress up a desktop browser to look like a phone.
        Websites have gotten very good at spotting the costume. Driftstack
        doesn't wear one. It runs a build of Apple's own WebKit — the
        software behind Safari on iPhone. So the iPhone behaviour is part of
        the browser itself, not a coat of paint on top. To a website, your
        session looks like a real iPhone.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-10 grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-3">
        <div class="card p-6">
          <p class="font-mono text-xs uppercase tracking-[0.2em] text-tk-accent-text">Measured, not guessed</p>
          <h3 class="mt-3 text-lg font-semibold text-tk-ink">Checked against real iPhones</h3>
          <p class="mt-2 text-sm leading-relaxed text-tk-ink-2">
            Websites quietly test how a browser draws pictures and graphics.
            Tiny differences are what give a fake away. Driftstack's answers
            are checked against real iPhones, check by check.
          </p>
        </div>
